<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  seoTitle={seoTitle}
  description={description}
  seoDescription={seoDescription}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Law career options after an LLB come down to ten routes, and each one sits behind a different gate: the AIBE for court practice, a state exam for the judiciary, CLAT PG or UGC NET for study and teaching, an ICSI exam for company secretary work, and no exam at all for compliance advisory.</strong>
    Pick the route by the next gate you can clear and the way the money works, not by whether your degree says BA LLB or BBA LLB. Choosing early lets you build a <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> in one route and move toward earlier financial freedom.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ten routes in three groups: licence-gated (court practice, own chambers, arbitration counsel), exam-gated (judiciary, company secretary, teaching) and skill-gated (compliance, legal tech, in-house, LPO).</li>
      <li>We lead with the routes that can grow through ownership: compliance advisory, then your own practice or boutique firm. Judiciary, top-firm hiring and teaching are rated Not so scalable because you must win stage after stage.</li>
      <li>The judiciary practice rule changed in May 2025 and again on 21 August 2026. A full-time salaried job can end your right to appear in court, and advocates cannot advertise.</li>
      <li><strong>Next step:</strong> shortlist two routes, note each one's next gate date, and test each with one real task before you spend on an LLM or coaching.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

