  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer on law career options</h2>

  <p>
    Most "career options" lists name the same jobs and stop. They do not tell you which exam or rule stands in front of each one, or which ones can grow into something you own.
  </p>

  <p>
    This page does that. It maps the <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options</a> open to a law graduate by degree stage, then labels each route as Scalable, Scalable with a business layer or Not so scalable. For pay by branch and the field-level view, read <a href="/blog/career-options/is-law-a-good-career-in-india/">is law a good career in India</a>. We leave out salary tables here on purpose.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Almost every route has a second filter hiding behind the first. Clearing the AIBE, a state exam or a firm interview gets you in. A client base, a promotion or an appointment is a separate, harder climb. Plan for both filters, not just the first.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="stage-ladder">What each law degree stage unlocks</h2>

  <p>
    Your stage decides your next legal gate. Find your row, then read the routes below with that gate in mind. A law degree from a Bar Council recognised college is a legal requirement for court practice, so this is a field where the degree matters. The problem is stopping at the degree with no skill built on top of it.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Where you stand</th>
          <th>What you have</th>
          <th>Your next gate</th>
          <th>What the first degree adds</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
