  <h2 id="own-practice">Own chambers or a boutique firm: the ownership path</h2>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>This is the route that scales through ownership.</strong> Your own practice lets you set fees, take on juniors as work grows, and build repeat and referred clients and a niche that no employer's pay band can match. Income then follows reputation and delegated capacity, not only the hours you personally bill.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The moves that separate a strong practice from a stalled one are specific:
  </p>

  <ul>
    {growthMoves.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p>
    It also has a catch. The first years can pay little, and the Bar Council rules limit how you can find clients. That is why the next section matters before you decide.
  </p>

  <h2 id="bar-council-rules">Bar Council rules on advertising and jobs</h2>

  <p>
    The Advocates Act and the Bar Council of India Rules shape every route that involves court practice. Two rules matter most for a career plan.
  </p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--gold">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What Rule 36 stops</div>
      <ul>
        {ruleStops.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-card compare-card--teal">
      <div class="compare-card__label">What still works</div>
      <ul>
        {ruleAllows.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    A breach can lead to misconduct proceedings before the State Bar Council under Section 35 of the Advocates Act. Reports of the 2025 enforcement mention suspension or cancellation of enrolment as possible outcomes, and notices to online platforms that listed advocates.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      The line between advertising and a normal professional profile is not clear in every case, and commentators call parts of the rule ambiguous. Do not guess. Ask your State Bar Council before you publish, print or post anything that could be read as a pitch.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    The second rule is Rule 49. It says an advocate cannot be a full-time salaried employee and keep practising. In October 2025 the Supreme Court relied on it when it held that in-house counsel are outside the definition of advocate for lawyer-client privilege. In plain terms: a salaried legal job and a court practice are two different lives. You can move from one to the other, but you cannot hold both.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-pays">Who pays, and how they find and trust you</h2>

  <p>
    Every route has a "who pays" layer that law school never teaches. Learn the basic version early.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>Who pays</th>
          <th>How they find and trust you</th>
          <th>First skill to learn</th>
          <th>One number to watch</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {whoPaysRows.map((item) => (
