  <h2 id="adjacent">When a neighbouring path is better</h2>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      If what really draws you is filings, governance and compliance rather than courts, the company secretary route can get you to similar work, and it opens a practising firm of your own. It will not open court practice or the judiciary. If you mainly want privacy and data work, a short privacy course plus a sample compliance pack may reach the first paid role faster than an LLM. Name the work you want, then pick the cheapest credential that unlocks it.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="money">Money: LLM, coaching and debt</h2>

  <p>
    Use roughly 10 percent of your family's total education budget as a starting benchmark for any degree fee, and treat anything near 20 percent as a serious caution that needs route-specific proof. This is a planning heuristic, not a law. The rest of the budget pays for living costs while you learn, books, tools, internships, exam fees and later skills.
  </p>

  <p>
    For an LLM, ask four questions: which route does it unlock, does that route need it (teaching does, court practice does not), what will it cost in fees plus a year without full income, and can you reach the same route through work experience? Avoid education debt unless a credential is legally required. Information is cheap here: official judgment portals, bare Acts, YouTube explainers by practising advocates and reputable short courses can test your interest first, as long as you judge the instructor, the update date and whether you finish with work you can show. None of them replaces the recognised degree and enrolment that court practice legally needs. <a href="/blog/college-degrees/certificate-courses-for-law-students/">Certificate courses for law students</a> compares paid options, and a pile of certificates is not a skill portfolio.
  </p>

